This throttle wraps a handler with `ms = 100`. The wrapper is called at 0, 50, 100, 150, 200 and 250 ms, measured from the first call (`Date.now()` itself is a large timestamp). How many times does the handler run, and what is the drawback?

```js
function throttle(fn, ms) {
  let last = 0;
  return function (...args) {
    const now = Date.now();
    if (now - last >= ms) {
      last = now;
      fn.apply(this, args);
    }
  };
}
```

